      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think comparing strlen is redundant when using strncmp.",
      "revId": "eb4fa40622e30b3299890c83aec245ca58a59c37",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4416ec41_9b0edeb2",
        "filename": "common/body_detection.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 278,
      "author": {
        "id": 1297197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-07T19:17:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Removing `strlen()` means `on` and `onf` are both treated the same, and enable the particular mode.\n\nThat\u0027s not any different than the behavior we have today, but also isn\u0027t entirely correct (and wouldn\u0027t catch typos). I would prefer to keep `strlen()` so we can handle the `onf`-type cases correctly.\n\nUsing `strcmp()` would solve this, but is unsafe since it could keep reading memory forever if the memory is not null-terminated, so we shouldn\u0027t use that.\n\nThoughts?",
      "parentUuid": "524f9e67_44b2d312",
      "revId": "eb4fa40622e30b3299890c83aec245ca58a59c37",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "90066233_258cfe0f",
        "filename": "common/body_detection.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 278,
      "author": {
        "id": 1390635
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-08T15:32:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Wouldn\u0027t `strncmp(argv[1], \"on\", strlen(\"on\")+1)` handle the `onf` case?",
      "parentUuid": "4416ec41_9b0edeb2",
      "revId": "eb4fa40622e30b3299890c83aec245ca58a59c37",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9a91d81d_a30fe99d",
        "filename": "common/body_detection.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 278,
      "author": {
        "id": 1297197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-08T18:14:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, I realized later that using `strlen()` is also relying on a null-terminator, so is just as safe as strcmp(). Using the `strlen+1` is a better choice.\n\nUsing \"+1\" should be safe, based on the `strlen()` implementation:\n\n\u003e The strlen() function calculates the length of the string pointed to by s, excluding the terminating null byte (\u0027\\0\u0027).\n\nThis means `strlen()+1` will allow `strncmp()` to read the null byte for comparison, but not past it into invalid memory.",
      "parentUuid": "90066233_258cfe0f",
      "revId": "eb4fa40622e30b3299890c83aec245ca58a59c37",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
